{"qid":"Q657","names":["Republic of Chad"],"title":"Republic of Chad","endonym":{"native":"جُمْهُورِيَّة تشاد","lang":"ar"},"capsule":"The Republic of Chad gained independence from France on August 11, 1960, and has since been defined by recurring civil wars, coups, and interference from neighbouring Sudan and Libya. President Hissène Habré's brutal rule (1982–90) was followed by Idriss Déby Itno's three-decade presidency, which ended when he was killed fighting rebels in April 2021, the day after winning a sixth term; his son Mahamat assumed power through a military council. Chad became an oil exporter in 2003, but revenues did not end chronic poverty or armed conflict in the Sahel borderlands.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Chad's international boundaries are defined by colonial treaties and are legally established, though in practice the frontiers with Sudan (especially around Darfur) and the Lake Chad basin have been contested or porous; the dataset geometry reflects internationally recognized borders.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Chad","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Chad"}],"britannica":"place/Chad","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Chad","rulers":[{"qid":"Q297812","name":"François Tombalbaye","start":1960,"end":1975,"image":"François Tombalbaye p1959.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fran%C3%A7ois%20Tombalbaye%20p1959.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"Public domain","artist":"Moshe Pridan","attributionRequired":false},"nativeName":"فرنسوا تومبالباي","nativeLang":"ar"},{"qid":"Q310886","name":"Hissène Habré","start":1978,"end":1979,